# Chat and AI

Canonical surface map: `docs/CHAT_AUDIT.md`. Read it before consolidating or adding chat UX.

## Separation of concerns

**Private assistant** (FAB / SSE / `ai_conversations`) and **shared thread** (WebSocket / `messages`) solve different problems—different persistence, different audience. Don’t merge transports without an explicit product decision.

**One context pipeline** builds system prompts, history, RAG, and explicit attachments for every LLM entry point. New triggers (thread @-mentions, workspace harness) plug into that pipeline— they don’t fork copy-pasted RAG blocks.

## Realtime and latency

Interactive channels stay responsive: heavy generation runs off the hot path (async task, stream endpoint, broadcast when ready). Never block a WebSocket loop on model latency.

AI messages in shared threads are first-class rows with the same broadcast semantics as human messages, visually distinct but equally durable.

## Memory and context

Thread memory is **collaborative** in shared channels—all participants’ messages in scope count, not only the requester’s private AI history.

**Explicit context** (files, topics, projects, past threads) is user-selected attachment, parallel to RAG—not a replacement for scope. Wire the same attachment model across composers that claim to support it.

Index conversations for retrieval via background work; indexing failures shouldn’t break send.

## Evolution

Deprecated pages and duplicate AI shells are not extension points—extend the supported surfaces or update the audit doc when retiring one.
